.TH SPARSETEST 1
.SH NAME
sparsetest \- test the sparse matrix module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sparsetest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Sparsetest
checks
.IR sparse (2)
against reference implementations written inside the test itself.
.PP
Every part of that module now has a fast version and an obvious one, and
the obvious one is the specification.
.B Newfrompattern
uses a marker array where it used to scan a list of neighbours per node;
.B find
binary-searches a row where it used to scan it; and
.B matvec
calls
.IR math (2)'s
.B spmv
where it used to loop in Limbo.
Each is several times faster, and each could be subtly wrong in a way
that still produces a plausible matrix rather than an obvious failure.
So the slow versions live here and the outputs are compared.
.PP
The element connectivity is random rather than a grid.
A structured mesh visits nodes in an order that hides an ordering
mistake, because the neighbours of a node arrive very nearly sorted
already; random clusters do not.
.PP
The pattern is compared to the reference
.I exactly
\(em every
.B rowptr
and
.B colidx
entry \(em and then each row is checked to be sorted and free of
duplicates, which is what
.B find
depends on.
Every entry the pattern claims must read back what was written to it,
and every column the pattern does not have must read as zero and swallow
a write without disturbing anything.
Finally
.B matvec
is compared against the row loop.
.PP
That last one is a tolerance and not an equality, because the C loop
contracts the multiply and add into a fused one and rounds differently:
a couple of units in the last place is expected, and anything larger is
not rounding.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sparsetest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sparse (2),
.IR math-linalg (2),
.IR krylovtest (1)
.SH BUGS
.B Dirichlet
is not tested.
.PP
One matrix, one shape of element, one size.
Nothing here exercises a matrix large enough for the pattern builder's
memory behaviour to matter, which is where its cost now is.
